[38;2;124;124;124mwith coefficients in F_p or some finite extension thereof?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about this carefully.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mA p-group is a finite group of order p^n. A unipotent representation over a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfield of characteristic p means a representation where every group element acts [0m[38;2;124;124;124mas a unipotent matrix (i.e., all eigenvalues are 1, equivalently the matrix [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mminus identity is nilpotent).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe question is whether every p-group admits a faithful unipotent representation[0m[38;2;124;124;124mover F_p (or a finite extension).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mKey facts:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m1. Every finite p-group can be embedded in the group of upper unitriangular [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmatrices UT_n(F_p) for some n. This is a classical result.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, let me think more carefully. The regular representation of a p-group [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mover F_p: since |G| = p^n, the group algebra F_p[G] is a local ring. The regular[0m[38;2;124;124;124mrepresentation decomposes, and the action of G on F_p[G] is by permutation [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmatrices. But permutation matrices are not unipotent in general... wait, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mactually they are, because in characteristic p, a permutation matrix of a p-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124melement is unipotent (since the order is a power of p, and x^{p^k} = I means (x-[0m[38;2;124;124;124mI)^{p^k} = 0 in characteristic p, so x-I is nilpotent, hence x is unipotent).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWait, but we need every element of G to act unipotently. In a p-group, every [0m
[38;2;124;124;124melement has order a power of p. If we have a representation ρ: G → GL_n(K) where[0m[38;2;124;124;124mK has characteristic p, then for any g ∈ G, g has order p^k for some k. Then [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mρ(g)^{p^k} = I. In characteristic p, (ρ(g) - I)^{p^k} = ρ(g)^{p^k} - I = 0 (by [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthe freshman's dream / Frobenius). So ρ(g) - I is nilpotent, hence ρ(g) is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124munipotent.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo ANY representation of a p-group over a field of characteristic p is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mautomatically unipotent! Because every element has p-power order, and in [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcharacteristic p, any matrix of p-power order is unipotent.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the question reduces to: does every p-group have a faithful representation [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mover F_p (or a finite extension)?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe answer is yes. Every finite group has a faithful representation over some [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfield. In fact, the regular representation is faithful. Over F_p, the regular [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mrepresentation of G is faithful (since F_p[G] is a free F_p-module of rank |G|, [0m[38;2;124;124;124mand G acts faithfully on it by left multiplication).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWait, is the regular representation over F_p faithful? Yes! The left regular [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mrepresentation maps g to the permutation matrix of left-multiplication-by-g. If [0m[38;2;124;124;124mg ≠ h, then g·1 ≠ h·1, so the permutations are different, hence the matrices are[0m[38;2;124;124;124mdifferent. So the regular representation is always faithful, regardless of the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfield.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo: every p-group G has a faithful representation over F_p (the regular [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mrepresentation), and since every element of G has p-power order and we're in [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcharacteristic p, every element acts unipotently. Therefore the regular [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mrepresentation is a faithful unipotent representation.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThat's the complete argument. Let me write it up cleanly.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, let me double-check the unipotency argument more carefully. A matrix M[0m[38;2;124;124;124mis unipotent if M - I is nilpotent, i.e., (M-I)^n = 0 for some n. Equivalently, [0m[38;2;124;124;124mall eigenvalues of M are 1.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIf ρ(g) has order p^k (since g has order dividing |G| = p^n), then ρ(g)^{p^k} = [0m[38;2;124;124;124mI. In characteristic p, by the Frobenius endomorphism property, (ρ(g) - I)^{p^k}[0m[38;2;124;124;124m= ρ(g)^{p^k} - I^{p^k} = I - I = 0. Wait, this isn't quite right. The Frobenius [0m[38;2;124;124;124msays (A+B)^{p^k} = A^{p^k} + B^{p^k} in characteristic p for commuting matrices.[0m[38;2;124;124;124mHere A = ρ(g) and B = -I commute. So (ρ(g) - I)^{p^k} = ρ(g)^{p^k} + (-I)^{p^k} [0m[38;2;124;124;124m= I + (-1)^{p^k} I.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIf p is odd, (-1)^{p^k} = -1, so we get I - I = 0. Good.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIf p = 2, (-1)^{2^k} = 1, so we get I + I = 2I = 0 in characteristic 2. Good.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo in both cases, (ρ(g) - I)^{p^k} = 0, hence ρ(g) - I is nilpotent, hence ρ(g) [0m[38;2;124;124;124mis unipotent.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mGreat, the argument works for all primes p.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the answer is YES, and the proof is straightforward:[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m1. The regular representation of any finite group G over any field K is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfaithful.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m2. For a p-group G over a field K of characteristic p, every element g ∈ G has [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mp-power order, so ρ(g)^{p^k} = I for some k.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m3. By the Frobenius property in characteristic p, (ρ(g) - I)^{p^k} = 0, so ρ(g) [0m[38;2;124;124;124mis unipotent.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m4. Therefore the regular representation over F_p is a faithful unipotent [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mrepresentation.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNo finite extension is even needed; F_p suffices.[0m

